<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murasaki Shikibu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 973–<a href="/years/1014">1014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Lady Murasaki, Fujiwara no Kaoruko</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murasaki Shikibu was a Japanese novelist, poet and lady-in-waiting at the Imperial court in the Heian period, author of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji</a>, widely considered one of the world's first novels, written between about 1000 and 1012. Born into a branch of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a> that had fallen to the middle and lower ranks of the aristocracy, the level of provincial governors, she learned the Chinese classics in her father's household, though Heian women were traditionally excluded from Chinese. In about 1005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-michinaga">Fujiwara no Michinaga</a> invited her to serve Empress Shōshi. Her personal name is unknown; most scholars date her death to 1014, others think she was alive in 1025.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murasaki-shikibu.jpg" alt="Murasaki Shikibu" width="330" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tosa Mitsuoki (1617 - 1691) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murasaki-Shikibu-composing-Genji-Monogatari.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Murasaki Shikibu</b> lived at the height of the Fujiwara clan's power, when Fujiwara no Michinaga was the regent in all but name and the most significant political figure of his day; she is believed to have formed the character of Genji partly through her experience of him.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…wrote instead in the new kana script; the result was much of the vernacular literature of the age: the Kagerō Nikki by “the mother of Michitsuna”, the Pillow Book of Sei Shōnagon, an attendant of the empress, and the Tale of Genji by <b>Murasaki Shikibu</b>, herself a Fujiwara.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
